        "Next comes <strong>ventricular systole</strong> (about 0.5 s): the ventricles contract strongly, generating pressure that slams the atrioventricular valves shut (producing the &lsquo;lub&rsquo; heart sound) and forces open the semilunar valves, pushing blood out into the aorta and pulmonary artery (stretching their walls) and generating the pulse felt throughout the arterial system; the atria, meanwhile, are relaxing (<strong>atrial diastole</strong>, by about 0.7 s).",
        "Finally, <strong>ventricular diastole</strong> follows: the ventricles relax, falling ventricular pressure lets the higher pressure in the aorta and pulmonary artery snap the semilunar valves shut (the &lsquo;dup&rsquo; sound), and the atrioventricular valves reopen as the ventricles&rsquo; pressure drops below that of the now-filling atria.",
